# Java

## Format and tooling

- Match the project's Java language level and build settings (Maven /
  Gradle / Android). Do not raise the bytecode target for one file.
- Format with the repo's Java formatter (google-java-format, Spotless, or
  IDE defaults). Do not hand-align beyond what the formatter keeps.
- Fix new compiler and lint findings on code you change. Do not
  `@SuppressWarnings` a finding to land the change unless the module
  already suppresses that warning for the same reason.
- Prefer the standard library and the project's existing dependencies. Do
  not add a library for a helper already on the classpath.

## Nullability and types

- Annotate nullability at public boundaries (`@Nullable` / `@NonNull`, or
  the project's chosen annotations). Prefer non-null return types.
- Prefer `Optional` only when the project already uses it for that kind of
  API — do not wrap every nullable field.
- Prefer immutable value types and enums over boolean pairs that encode
  variants.
- Prefer `final` locals and fields unless reassignment is required.
- Keep generics honest. Do not use raw types.

## Exceptions and APIs

- Prefer checked exceptions only when the project already uses them for
  that boundary; otherwise prefer unchecked domain exceptions the caller
  can handle.
- Do not swallow an exception with an empty `catch`. Log or wrap with
  context, or document why it is safe to ignore.
- Close resources with try-with-resources. Do not leave streams or cursors
  open across an early return.
- Prefer the project's existing DI / Android component patterns. Do not
  introduce a second injection framework for one class.

## Android and packaging

- Prefer the project's existing UI stack (Views or the shared Compose
  interop path). Do not introduce a second UI toolkit for one screen.
- Do main-thread UI updates only on the main thread. Prefer the project's
  existing executor / coroutine / WorkManager path for background work —
  do not spawn unmanaged threads for one call.
- Keep packages named for what they provide, not `util` or `common`.
- One primary type per file when the file would otherwise mix networking,
  persistence, and presentation.

## Tests

- Prefer the project's test stack (JUnit, instrumentation). Ship a unit
  test for a new exported behavior in the same change
  (`code-unit-testing`).
- Cover the failure path and one success path. Prefer fakes or the
  project's existing test doubles over a heavyweight mock for a single
  call.
- Do not `Thread.sleep` to wait for async work. Use the project's
  existing IdlingResource, coroutine test, or latch helper.
